WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:22.500
Heating systems keep people alive in winter. You need to know them cold.

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:45.000
A gas furnace burns natural gas or propane to heat air. The sequence: thermostat
calls for heat, draft inducer starts, pressure switch closes, igniter glows at
1800F+, gas valve opens, burners ignite, flame sensor proves flame, heat

00:00:45.000 --> 00:01:07.500
Here are the key terms. Write these down.

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:30.000
Heat Exchanger: Metal chambers transferring combustion heat to room air. A crack
leaks CO.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:52.500
Flame Sensor: Thin metal rod proving flame by conducting microamps. Dirty flame
sensor is the number one no-heat call.

00:01:52.500 --> 00:02:15.000
Here's why this matters on the job: Gas furnace calls are winter bread and
butter. Diagnosing no-heat in 15 minutes keeps you busy all season.

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:37.500
Watch out for these: Read control board LED fault codes FIRST. And — Dirty flame
sensor is the most common no-heat cause

00:02:37.500 --> 00:03:00.000
Take a minute before you move on. What's the one thing from this lesson you need
to remember on a service call?

